WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.320
The next housing crash is coming.&nbsp;
Which according to the US Senate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.960
may cause “a collapse in property values with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.640
potential to trigger a full-scale financial&nbsp;
crisis similar to what occurred in 2008.”

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.680
That’s… scary. But, this crisis is&nbsp;
different. It’s not starting at the banks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.760
or supply… but something else… Home insurance.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:28.640
Americans now pay a record $209 a month&nbsp;
just to insure their home. Nearly 80%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.880
more than in 2020. And one year of those&nbsp;
price hikes was linked to around 149,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:40.160
more mortgages falling behind on payments.&nbsp;
And the worst is yet to come. “there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.080
going to be regions of the country&nbsp;
where you can’t get a mortgage.”

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:50.880
There’s a projected $1.47 trillion erosion&nbsp;
in US home value over the next 30 years. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:59.600
how might insurance cause a housing crisis?

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.640
If you don’t follow housing,&nbsp;
or haven’t bought a house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.080
you might wonder “why does insurance&nbsp;
matter?” Well, it does. A lot.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.920
Most mortgage holders pay the insurance&nbsp;
premium, the monthly insurance fee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.960
inside their monthly payment to the bank, with the&nbsp;
mortgage payment, aka the principal, the interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.560
and the property taxes. You pay it to the bank in&nbsp;
a bucket, and the bank hands it to the insurer.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.040
But insurance is a deceptively important&nbsp;
part of the housing market. You can’t get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.360
a mortgage… without home insurance.&nbsp;
Lenders require insurance to protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.800
the house the loan is secured on. So no&nbsp;
insurance… no house. With this in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.720
let’s look at the first issue, which is&nbsp;
how expensive insurance premiums are.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.160
Insurance is now 9.6% of the average&nbsp;
mortgage payment. And across the board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:53.280
this is going up. And quickly. Insurance premiums&nbsp;
went up in 95% of U.S. ZIP codes between 2021 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.240
2024, and in that time, “annual insurance&nbsp;
premiums for a typical homeowner increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:04.560
by an average of $648 across the country.&nbsp;
This translates to a 24 percent increase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.160
11 percent higher than the cumulative&nbsp;
inflation over the same period.”

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.360
But there are pockets of these, where it gets&nbsp;
pretty ridiculous. North Dakota, Minnesota,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:19.760
Arkansas, Nebraska, and Kentucky all saw&nbsp;
jumps over 30%, Louisiana, Pennsylvania,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:26.880
and Arizona saw jumps of 40%, Illinois&nbsp;
jumped 50%, and Utah jumped as much as 59%!

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:33.520
Some metro areas, were the worst, though. Salt&nbsp;
Lake City, for example, jumped 62% in this period.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:40.880
But one of the worst, by far, is California,&nbsp;
where from 2020 to 2026, premiums rose 84%.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:47.120
So why have premiums gone up so much? A piece&nbsp;
of the puzzle, is that houses just cost more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:52.320
to rebuild. Rising construction costs explain&nbsp;
slightly over half of the rise in premiums from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:03:01.360
2020 to 2023. Rebuilding a home cost 43.9% more in&nbsp;
October 2024 than in October 2019, and from 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:09.200
it’s over 63%. A big part is lumber, with an&nbsp;
average increase of 43.8% since Oct. 2014.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.160
But, this isn’t nearly enough to explain the&nbsp;
jumps. A big reason, is that insurers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.120
charging more for disaster risk. Wildfires&nbsp;
and storms drive a lot of the increase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.800
which is why California is so much higher. We’re&nbsp;
seeing more extreme weather and more wildfires.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.440
But… a lot of these jumps aren’t just on&nbsp;
the East or West Coast, but inland. Severe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.640
convective storms are small in footprint but&nbsp;
frequent, and of all the homeowner claims these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:41.120
storms cause each year, roughly half to 80% are&nbsp;
for hail damage rather than wind or tornadoes.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:47.760
Something deeper is driving this. Not the storms&nbsp;
or wildfires. And this impacts something else:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.800
reinsurance, which helps us&nbsp;
understand why so many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.400
can’t even get insurance anymore.&nbsp;
State Farm named reinsurance costs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:00.400
as one reason for dropping 72,000&nbsp;
California policies. So what is it?

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.680
Well, it’s basically the insurance…&nbsp;
for insurance companies. Most claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.840
trickle in, which the insurer can&nbsp;
handle. Out of a thousand homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:15.280
maybe only a handful file claims. But in a&nbsp;
disaster, like a bad hailstorm, wildfires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:20.240
or a hurricane, thousands of the insurer’s&nbsp;
customers can be hit in the same day. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.200
storm can bring more claims in a week than&nbsp;
the insurer collected in premiums over years.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.360
So, insurance companies, who might be more&nbsp;
regional to one state, have reinsurance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.000
for global coverage, not just in&nbsp;
America. Yeah, the insurance companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.320
have their own insurance, and when the&nbsp;
reinsurers raise prices… it can cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.520
problems. Insurers pass their reinsurance&nbsp;
bill straight on to customers. By 2023&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.400
the jump had added nearly $300 a year to&nbsp;
premiums in the most exposed ZIP codes.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.360
But what happens, when insurers can’t afford&nbsp;
it? What happens when hundreds of thousands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:05:07.360
or millions of homes, aren’t even worth insuring?&nbsp;
The answer, may lead to a new housing crisis…

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:15.040
In March 2024, Cindy Picos had her home insurance&nbsp;
dropped. Why? Well, CSAA Insurance said her roof&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:21.280
had “lived its life expectancy.” Yet, they hadn’t&nbsp;
visited her home. They had dropped her insurance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.520
just from an aerial photo. And… they&nbsp;
refused to let her even see the photo.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.560
Insurance companies have stopped&nbsp;
deploying people to inspect homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.760
and will instead deploy drones, airplanes, even&nbsp;
high-altitude balloons to take photos. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:39.360
not just niche parts of the US either. These&nbsp;
programs can cover 99% of the US population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.400
“Not every company's using this kind of&nbsp;
technology, but, they will eventually.”

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.400
Cindy paid for an independent inspection, and&nbsp;
sure enough, the roof was in great shape. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:56.240
10 years. But when this was submitted, CSAA&nbsp;
Insurance… just refused to change its decision.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:01.040
And some of these are weird. Another homeowner,&nbsp;
Marilyn Smith, had their insurance dropped…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:07.360
because in the photo… the pool was drained?&nbsp;
Yeah. They had emptied it to conserve water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:12.560
during a drought, and to save on maintenance.&nbsp;
Perfectly reasonable. But according to AAA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:17.360
the pool showed signs of “deferred maintenance.”&nbsp;
So… their home insurance was dropped.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.840
“She just flat out said because the pool&nbsp;
was empty. I don't understand what their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.440
problem is. Because you empty a pool and you're&nbsp;
saving on water. I think I was in so much shock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.200
I couldn't believe it. I mean, we&nbsp;
were both in shock. Because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.280
pool is empty. What's that got to do&nbsp;
with canceling your home insurance?”

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:41.520
This is becoming shockingly common. And of&nbsp;
course, this comes with huge privacy concerns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:47.040
but you might be thinking “what does this have&nbsp;
to do with a housing crisis?” Well, as you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:53.120
have noticed, insurers are getting very, very&nbsp;
picky about who they’ll insure. Now, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:58.320
do everything right, and still get dropped.&nbsp;
Without making a claim or missing a payment.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.960
The US Treasury looked at 246 million&nbsp;
home insurance policies from 2018 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.920
2022. A policy being one home's insurance&nbsp;
contract for one year. “Consumers in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.880
highest risk ZIP codes faced higher policy&nbsp;
nonrenewal rates, with average nonrenewal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:17.920
rates about 80 percent higher than those&nbsp;
in the lowest risk ZIP Codes. Moreover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:21.920
average nonrenewal rates increased more in&nbsp;
the highest risk areas than in the lowest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:27.120
risk areas over this period, which indicates&nbsp;
that consumers faced decreasing availability.”

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.360
So, what does that mean? If&nbsp;
you live in riskier areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:35.120
places more likely to have wildfires&nbsp;
or coastal areas… you’re nearly twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:39.120
as likely to have your insurer drop&nbsp;
you, compared to safer areas. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:44.560
if you live somewhere where you need insurance,&nbsp;
you’re more likely to have no insurance.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:49.920
Between 2018 and 2022, Florida saw&nbsp;
a 280 percent increase in nonrenewal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.000
rates. And if you aren’t dropped,&nbsp;
your premiums are getting higher.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:08:00.560
In 2024, State Farm dropped coverage for 30,000&nbsp;
homes and 42,000 apartments in California. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.520
I find State Farm’s reasoning interesting.&nbsp;
“This decision was not made lightly and only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.480
after careful analysis of State&nbsp;
Farm General’s financial health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:11.920
which continues to be impacted by&nbsp;
inflation, catastrophe exposure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:16.480
reinsurance costs and the limitations of&nbsp;
working within decades-old regulations”

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:23.440
From 2017 to 2024, insurers made no profit on&nbsp;
home insurance in seven of the eight years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:29.600
In 2024, homeowner insurers saw a loss&nbsp;
of $1.3 billion. And a big part of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:34.880
was not only the cost of rebuilding homes,&nbsp;
but also those rising reinsurance costs.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:42.080
But that’s changed. In 2025, home insurers&nbsp;
made $16.5 billion in profit, the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:50.080
since 2019. And that’s not all. Reinsurance&nbsp;
prices are down 22% from the 2024 peak. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:56.080
why are premiums still rising? Why are&nbsp;
so many homes being dropped? Well, risk.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.280
Insurers are trying to carry less risk after&nbsp;
years of losses and expensive reinsurance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:05.200
They are also, trying to reduce cost.&nbsp;
Aerial photos allow them to be cheap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:09.920
and picky at scale. Which, of course,&nbsp;
they use AI to analyze these photos.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:14.240
Millions of homeowners across the United&nbsp;
States are being denied or priced out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:20.000
property insurance coverage. So what happens to&nbsp;
all of those who get dropped by private insurance?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:25.920
Well, many end up on state-created insurers of&nbsp;
last resort. But this is higher-priced, less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:31.920
comprehensive insurance coverage. And, it’s not&nbsp;
just the riskiest homes ending up on these plans.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:37.040
“A new phenomenon is emerging: Californians’&nbsp;
dependence on the FAIR Plan is now showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:41.040
up with mortgages in moderate- and&nbsp;
low- wildfire risk zip codes at twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:45.840
the rate of its overall market share. The&nbsp;
problem is bigger than most people think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.880
and it's going to get worse&nbsp;
before it gets better.”

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.520
But… what about people who just&nbsp;
aren’t insured altogether? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:56.560
this, the rising premiums and&nbsp;
more people on state insurers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:07.760
of last resort, is why we seem to be&nbsp;
approaching an “insurability crisis.”

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:14.160
“One in thirteen American homeowners are&nbsp;
uninsured—approximately 7.4% – living in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:19.280
about 6.1 million homes.” According to&nbsp;
the Consumer Federation’s 2024 study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:23.440
uninsured homeowners are concentrated among&nbsp;
lower earners, owners of manufactured homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:25.680
and people who inherited their home.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.480
The total number could be even higher though.&nbsp;
Other estimates put between “7 percent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:36.400
13 percent of all homes in the country as&nbsp;
uninsured, and that number is on the rise.”

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:39.360
So how does a rising uninsured housing market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:43.360
lead to a financial crisis?&nbsp;
Well, let’s break it down.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:48.240
In the 2008 financial crisis, millions of&nbsp;
homeowners became “underwater,” meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:52.880
they owed more than their homes were worth.&nbsp;
This made refinancing or selling impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.240
or very unattractive, because&nbsp;
it doesn’t cover the mortgage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:59.680
and you’d still owe money. So defaults shot up.

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:03.760
We’re seeing something similar… just&nbsp;
in a different order. If no one will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:08.080
insure your home, and no one will buy&nbsp;
it… what is your asset really worth?

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:12.560
“If those homes become uninsurable&nbsp;
and unmarketable, the values of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:17.040
homes will plummet, perhaps to&nbsp;
zero. Unlike recent experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:21.440
homeowners will have no expectation that&nbsp;
the values of their homes will ever recover”

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:26.320
So, prices fall. And they already&nbsp;
are. “The reinsurance shock reduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:29.840
2023 home values by an average of $8,400”

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:33.440
Texas and Florida have seen home&nbsp;
prices fall more dramatically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:37.600
and account for 39% of all&nbsp;
underwater homes nationwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:45.280
“The number of borrowers who are underwater was up&nbsp;
44% year-over-year at the end of June to 813,000”.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:50.320
But it’s not just houses that can’t be&nbsp;
mortgaged. It’s people trying to get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:55.040
mortgage too. “A $1,000 increase in annual&nbsp;
property insurance premiums increases the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:01.920
probability of mortgage refinancing denial”,&nbsp;
by 15%. New purchases also fall by 7.8 percent.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:06.720
It also means people fail to pay their mortgage&nbsp;
more. “The average single-family mortgage holder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:14.080
now pays a record $209 per month for insurance,&nbsp;
nearly 80% more than at the start of 2020.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:18.800
Researchers at the Dallas Fed tracked millions&nbsp;
of mortgages and found that when insurance bills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:23.920
went up, more people fell behind on their&nbsp;
home loan. The typical premium rise in 2022&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:30.560
and 2023 alone was linked to around 149,000 extra&nbsp;
Americans missing mortgage payments within a year.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:34.640
About one in ten buyers and sellers&nbsp;
now hit insurance problems too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:40.000
many of which caused a deal to fall through.&nbsp;
In California, it’s one in seven sellers.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:44.720
The Federal Reserve Chair Jerome Powell&nbsp;
told the Senate Banking Committee “in 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:49.040
or 15 years there are going to be regions of&nbsp;
the country where you can’t get a mortgage.”

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:52.720
And something we have to remember,&nbsp;
is that insurance, in many cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:57.600
isn’t just for the homeowner. It’s for the&nbsp;
bank. It’s why you can’t get a mortgage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:02.000
without home insurance, to protect the&nbsp;
bank's loan if the house is destroyed.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:05.200
Without insurance, the loss moves&nbsp;
from the insurer to the lender,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:08.480
be it mortgages themselves,&nbsp;
or mortgage-backed securities.

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:13.040
“Since mortgages and mortgage-backed&nbsp;
securities are central to the financial sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:17.600
increased delinquencies can destabilize&nbsp;
financial institutions. Thus, climate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:22.320
change’s impact on insurance rates represents an&nbsp;
emerging threat to broader financial stability.”

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:26.800
“This is predicted to cascade into plunging&nbsp;
property values in communities where insurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:32.160
becomes impossible to find or prohibitively&nbsp;
expensive—a collapse in property values with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:37.680
the potential to trigger a full-scale financial&nbsp;
crisis similar to what occurred in 2008…”

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:42.240
Houses aren’t the only market which is going&nbsp;
through a crisis. Another surprising one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:48.560
is used cars. Ever since the pandemic, something&nbsp;
in the market broke, and has never been fixed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:54.400
Affordable used cars are disappearing, and&nbsp;
to find out why, watch this video next.

